00:10:33,860 --> 00:10:36,100
how good were those prices?

257
00:10:36,100 --> 00:10:37,620
How good were those prices?

258
00:10:37,620 --> 00:10:40,260
Of course, this always means that for any one event,

259
00:10:40,260 --> 00:10:42,940
it's either going to be worth zero or a dollar at the end.

260
00:10:42,940 --> 00:10:44,500
And then people say, see,

261
00:10:44,500 --> 00:10:46,780
the market is wrong no matter what the market said.

262
00:10:46,780 --> 00:10:49,180
Yeah. So, if the market price was 80 cents,

263
00:10:49,180 --> 00:10:50,900
and it happens, and they say, well,

264
00:10:50,900 --> 00:10:52,780
it said 80, but actually it happened.

265
00:10:52,780 --> 00:10:54,580
And if it doesn't happen, it said 80,

266
00:10:54,580 --> 00:10:56,020
but it didn't happen. So,

267
00:10:56,020 --> 00:10:57,820
there's a, either there's a 20 cent gap,

268
00:10:57,820 --> 00:10:58,860
or there's an 80 cent gap.

269
00:10:58,860 --> 00:11:01,220
Either way, markets were way off, right?

270
00:11:01,220 --> 00:11:02,580
You know, like the fair standard,

271
00:11:02,580 --> 00:11:06,420
and you have to collect a large enough number of events

272
00:11:06,420 --> 00:11:08,740
where you can actually get a continuous number,

273
00:11:08,740 --> 00:11:10,180
or whether it was, otherwise, of course,

274
00:11:10,180 --> 00:11:12,140
every event after the fact, either happened or didn't.

275
00:11:12,140 --> 00:11:14,900
So, it's not going to help you understand the world.

276
00:11:14,900 --> 00:11:17,700
In fact, if you base your whole view about betting markets

277
00:11:17,700 --> 00:11:20,580
based upon that, it will lead to great confusion.

278
00:11:21,460 --> 00:11:24,380
So, you know, a lot of people after Brexit and Trump,

279
00:11:24,380 --> 00:11:26,100
just said betting markets are worthless.

280
00:11:26,100 --> 00:11:28,260
Why? Because two really high profile

281
00:11:28,260 --> 00:11:29,900
betting markets were off.

282
00:11:30,980 --> 00:11:33,500
Yeah, but what about all the ones that work?

283
00:11:33,500 --> 00:11:34,700
Of course, you know, so look,

284
00:11:34,700 --> 00:11:37,340
if betting markets say 15%,

285
00:11:37,340 --> 00:11:40,740
then it should happen 15% of the time, right?

286
00:11:41,620 --> 00:11:44,860
So, if things the market said were 85% likely never happened,

287
00:11:44,860 --> 00:11:46,300
that would be a problem too.

288
00:11:48,420 --> 00:11:49,540
All right.

289
00:11:49,540 --> 00:11:51,860
Okay. So, a lot of the stuff is just what you expect.

290
00:11:51,860 --> 00:11:52,940
Everything's fine.

291
00:11:52,940 --> 00:11:54,980
The fission markets analysis is working.

292
00:11:54,980 --> 00:11:59,300
But, as usual, when everything seems to be working about fine,

293
00:11:59,300 --> 00:12:02,700
there are people who say, yeah, about,

294
00:12:02,700 --> 00:12:04,860
but what about the exceptions?

295
00:12:04,860 --> 00:12:06,100
What about the counter examples?

296
00:12:06,100 --> 00:12:08,900
And here are some that have been talked about.

297
00:12:08,900 --> 00:12:12,540
So, the best known counter example is called long shot bias.

298
00:12:13,540 --> 00:12:16,140
Best known counter example to this is long shot bias.

299
00:12:16,140 --> 00:12:21,020
All right.

300
00:12:21,020 --> 00:12:26,020
And what long shot bias says is that expected returns,

301
00:12:26,020 --> 00:12:28,260
specifically in horse racing, is where it's best studied,

302
00:12:28,260 --> 00:12:30,860
although people will generalize it to others,

303
00:12:30,860 --> 00:12:32,540
expected returns increase in the probability

304
00:12:32,540 --> 00:12:33,740
of the horse winning.

305
00:12:33,740 --> 00:12:37,100
So, a horse that has a 90% chance of winning,

306
00:12:37,100 --> 00:12:39,380
you have a higher average payoff than for a horse

307
00:12:39,380 --> 00:12:41,940
that has only a 10% chance of winning.

308
00:12:41,940 --> 00:12:44,340
You may say, well, of course, because the 90% chance wins

309
00:12:44,340 --> 00:12:45,780
90% of the time.

310
00:12:45,780 --> 00:12:48,260
Yes, but you have to remember, what happens,

311
00:12:48,260 --> 00:12:50,900
if the horse is expected to win, what happens to the payoffs

312
00:12:50,900 --> 00:12:52,980
at the betting track?

313
00:12:52,980 --> 00:12:54,220
They go down.

314
00:12:54,220 --> 00:12:55,220
Right?

315
00:12:55,220 --> 00:12:57,980
In fact, the way that racing markets work is normally what's

316
00:12:57,980 --> 00:13:02,740
called paributral betting, where essentially you adjust the odds

317
00:13:02,740 --> 00:13:05,260
so that you balance out the total amount of money bet.

318
00:13:05,260 --> 00:13:07,940
So, if there's 10 times as much money bet on the favorite horse

319
00:13:07,940 --> 00:13:09,740
as on the disfavorite horse,

320
00:13:09,740 --> 00:13:12,380
then you basically set the odds at 10 to 1.

321
00:13:12,380 --> 00:13:14,420
And that way, you will always be,

322
00:13:14,420 --> 00:13:16,700
the track will always be able to pay.

323
00:13:16,700 --> 00:13:19,220
Of course, in reality, these are tracked out of commission.

324
00:13:19,220 --> 00:13:20,220
Right?

325
00:13:20,220 --> 00:13:23,500
So, if people want to put $1,000 on the favored to win

326
00:13:23,500 --> 00:13:25,580
and $100 on the disfavored to win,

327
00:13:25,580 --> 00:13:27,540
then if you do 10 to 1 odds,

328
00:13:27,540 --> 00:13:30,540
this means that if the disfavored horse wins,

329
00:13:30,540 --> 00:13:32,420
you take the $1,000 and give it to the people

330
00:13:32,420 --> 00:13:34,340
who bet on the disfavored horse.

331
00:13:34,340 --> 00:13:37,860
And if the favored horse wins, you take the $100

332
00:13:37,860 --> 00:13:39,660
and you spread it around all the people

333
00:13:39,660 --> 00:13:41,940
that bet on the favored horse to win.

334
00:13:41,940 --> 00:13:44,100
Right? So, that means the people that bet on the favored horse,

335
00:13:44,100 --> 00:13:46,420
they only get, what would that be?

336
00:13:46,420 --> 00:13:48,540
So, they get, you know, they get their,

337
00:13:48,540 --> 00:13:50,820
they get back their initial money plus 10 cents.

338
00:13:50,820 --> 00:13:53,180
Whereas the people bet on the disfavored horse win,

339
00:13:53,180 --> 00:13:56,020
then they get back their money plus 10 times what they bet.

340
00:13:58,580 --> 00:14:01,220
Okay. Now, why would this be?

341
00:14:01,220 --> 00:14:03,780
Well, the folk wisdom at the betting track,

342
00:14:03,780 --> 00:14:06,140
which Thaler is happy to relay,

343
00:14:06,140 --> 00:14:08,140
is that there are a lot of people

344
00:14:08,140 --> 00:14:11,460
who want to finish in the money, in the money.

345
00:14:11,460 --> 00:14:13,740
Right? So, a lot of people want to make sure

346
00:14:13,740 --> 00:14:15,180
that they leave with as much,

347
00:14:15,180 --> 00:14:17,380
at least as much money as they arrived with.

348
00:14:17,380 --> 00:14:18,700
All right.

349
00:14:18,700 --> 00:14:21,700
Now, if you have lost a bunch of times during the day,

350
00:14:21,700 --> 00:14:25,780
as you normally will, and you are down to the last few races,

351
00:14:25,780 --> 00:14:27,620
what is the only, you know, you go and bet safe

352
00:14:27,620 --> 00:14:29,300
on those last races,

353
00:14:29,300 --> 00:14:32,540
you will still walk away having lost money.

354
00:14:32,540 --> 00:14:34,900
So, what is your only hope for not having

355
00:14:34,900 --> 00:14:37,380
to explain why you gambled away your whole paycheck?

356
00:14:37,380 --> 00:14:38,700
Bet on the three-legged horse.

357
00:14:38,700 --> 00:14:40,580
Yes. Bet on the three-legged horse.

358
00:14:40,580 --> 00:14:43,940
And then, maybe you lose everything,

359
00:14:43,940 --> 00:14:47,860
but maybe you go and then there's a small chance you win.

360
00:14:47,860 --> 00:14:49,540
And there's a bunch of people who think this way,

361
00:14:49,540 --> 00:14:52,020
who go and bet on the three-legged horse

362
00:14:52,020 --> 00:14:55,420
to an even greater deal, like they pour that money in.

363
00:14:55,420 --> 00:14:58,300
This goes and actually messes up the odds,

364
00:14:58,300 --> 00:14:59,540
because there's too much money going

365
00:14:59,540 --> 00:15:01,180
on that three-legged horse.

366
00:15:01,180 --> 00:15:05,260
And that means that it puts the balance closer.

367
00:15:05,260 --> 00:15:06,500
And then, since three-legged horses

368
00:15:06,500 --> 00:15:08,820
on average really have very low chances of winning,

369
00:15:08,820 --> 00:15:11,820
this means that even when you do win in the long shot,

370
00:15:11,820 --> 00:15:16,820
you actually wind up getting a worse payoff on average

371
00:15:18,100 --> 00:15:19,860
than if you bet on the safe horse.

372
00:15:22,460 --> 00:15:24,860
So, it's a loss aversion story then?

373
00:15:24,860 --> 00:15:28,020
Yeah, I mean, it is similar to loss aversion,

374
00:15:28,020 --> 00:15:30,300
because it's a little bit more specific,

375
00:15:30,300 --> 00:15:32,380
because there's this very specific thing of,

376
00:15:32,380 --> 00:15:34,820
I want to make sure that I have greater than or equal

377
00:15:34,820 --> 00:15:37,020
to the amount of money that I came with.

378
00:15:37,020 --> 00:15:39,500
So, it's one where there's kind of an insensitivity

379
00:15:39,500 --> 00:15:42,420
between losing everything and losing 90%.

380
00:15:42,420 --> 00:15:44,820
But yeah, like I said.

381
00:15:44,820 --> 00:15:46,420
So, the best time to bet on horses

382
00:15:46,420 --> 00:15:48,580
is just bet on the healthy one at the end?

383
00:15:48,580 --> 00:15:50,580
Yeah, yeah, bet on the safe horse at the end.

384
00:15:50,580 --> 00:15:53,500
And I think Thaler actually has further documentation

385
00:15:53,500 --> 00:15:54,660
in support of that.

386
00:15:54,660 --> 00:15:55,900
Now, a key thing to remember is that

387
00:15:55,900 --> 00:15:57,780
since the commission is substantial,

388
00:15:57,780 --> 00:15:58,700
I think even Thaler will say,

389
00:15:58,700 --> 00:16:00,940
you still on average lose money.

390
00:16:00,940 --> 00:16:03,980
So, you have to actually enjoy being in the horse races,

391
00:16:03,980 --> 00:16:05,860
or the thrill of betting, or something

392
00:16:05,860 --> 00:16:07,020
to make it worthwhile.

393
00:16:09,460 --> 00:16:13,060
There's one casino where actually the odds

394
00:16:13,060 --> 00:16:15,140
are in the gambler's favor,

395
00:16:15,140 --> 00:16:17,380
and that is, of course, the stock market.

396
00:16:17,380 --> 00:16:19,300
So, that is the,

397
00:16:19,300 --> 00:16:20,140
the way this is,

398
00:16:21,300 --> 00:16:22,380
is a slogan, it seems like,

399
00:16:22,380 --> 00:16:23,940
if I ran the stock exchange,

400
00:16:23,940 --> 00:16:25,980
I would actually put this in front of,

401
00:16:25,980 --> 00:16:27,900
like put this on our main webpage,

402
00:16:27,900 --> 00:16:30,620
and I would carve it into the stock exchange,

403
00:16:30,620 --> 00:16:32,940
and like, the New York Stock Exchange,

404
00:16:32,940 --> 00:16:34,540
the casino where the odds are in your favor.

405
00:16:34,540 --> 00:16:35,980
Exclamation point.

406
00:16:35,980 --> 00:16:36,940
Right, and it seems like,

407
00:16:36,940 --> 00:16:38,500
it seems like a really good slogan to me,

408
00:16:38,500 --> 00:16:40,380
but I guess they know what they're doing,

409
00:16:40,380 --> 00:16:43,220
so, I mean, I guess people don't wanna hear it's a casino,

410
00:16:44,060 --> 00:16:45,420
because they think of casinos as places

411
00:16:45,420 --> 00:16:46,260
where you lose money.

412
00:16:46,260 --> 00:16:49,020
No, no, no, but it's like a casino except you make money.

413
00:16:49,020 --> 00:16:49,860
Like, eh.

414
00:16:50,980 --> 00:16:52,940
That sounds like a place where you lose money.

415
00:16:52,940 --> 00:16:53,780
All right.

416
00:16:56,140 --> 00:17:00,020
All right, and then another example of something similar

417
00:17:00,020 --> 00:17:02,700
is in lottery betting.

418
00:17:02,700 --> 00:17:04,820
All right, so there's, conventionalism says

419
00:17:04,820 --> 00:17:07,460
lotteries are just a tax instrument.

420
00:17:07,460 --> 00:17:09,820
All right, so someone goes and buys a lottery ticket,

421
00:17:09,820 --> 00:17:10,820
and like, what an idiot.

422
00:17:10,820 --> 00:17:11,780
Why would you ever do that

423
00:17:11,780 --> 00:17:14,540
when you could say buy a share of Apple?

424
00:17:14,540 --> 00:17:15,900
Something like that.

425
00:17:15,900 --> 00:17:17,020
All right.

426
00:17:17,020 --> 00:17:19,660
But Thaler points out there actually are ways

427
00:17:19,660 --> 00:17:22,900
where you can get up to a positive expected return.

428
00:17:22,900 --> 00:17:25,260
Right, and how so?

429
00:17:25,260 --> 00:17:26,460
Well, there's a lot of people,

430
00:17:26,460 --> 00:17:28,340
so they'll be like, if there's a lottery

431
00:17:28,340 --> 00:17:30,780
where you're allowed to pick your number,

432
00:17:30,780 --> 00:17:32,780
and again, if it's this kind of parimutuel betting

433
00:17:32,780 --> 00:17:35,140
where the way that they handle out the payoffs

434
00:17:35,140 --> 00:17:37,380
is that if a lot of people pick a number,

435
00:17:37,380 --> 00:17:39,100
they have to split the payoff,

436
00:17:39,100 --> 00:17:41,300
but if only one person picks the number,

437
00:17:41,300 --> 00:17:43,220
then they get the whole payoff,

438
00:17:43,220 --> 00:17:45,100
then there's a very simple strategy

439
00:17:45,100 --> 00:17:48,540
for dramatically increasing your payoff,

440
00:17:48,540 --> 00:17:51,700
and that is you just wanna pick unpopular numbers.

441
00:17:51,700 --> 00:17:55,340
Right now, say, well, like, what is an unpopular number?

442
00:17:55,340 --> 00:17:57,180
Well, again, so probably like superstitions

443
00:17:57,180 --> 00:18:00,620
about like seven being unlucky and 13 being lucky.

444
00:18:00,620 --> 00:18:02,460
That's one place to start,

445
00:18:02,460 --> 00:18:04,060
and again, do you wanna pick lucky numbers

446
00:18:04,060 --> 00:18:05,860
or unlucky numbers?

447
00:18:05,860 --> 00:18:07,660
Unlucky numbers, you wanna pick the numbers

448
00:18:07,660 --> 00:18:10,380
that your society believes send you to hell.

449
00:18:10,380 --> 00:18:12,100
Those are the good numbers,

450
00:18:12,100 --> 00:18:14,340
because your society's wrong, all right?

451
00:18:14,340 --> 00:18:16,540
All that it leads to is earthly riches.

452
00:18:16,540 --> 00:18:19,140
All right, but then other things,

453
00:18:19,140 --> 00:18:20,180
there's a lot of people who do things

454
00:18:20,180 --> 00:18:21,580
like betting their birthday.

455
00:18:22,540 --> 00:18:26,560
So bet numbers that are not anybody's birthday.

456
00:18:26,560 --> 00:18:31,180
Bet like 1332, and then, let's see,

457
00:18:31,180 --> 00:18:33,460
right now, hardly anyone, but anybody else,

458
00:18:33,460 --> 00:18:37,140
so like bet like 1332.01, right?

459
00:18:37,140 --> 00:18:38,100
Because there's hardly anyone buying,

460
00:18:38,100 --> 00:18:40,420
or actually, even back in, but now bet like 17.

461
00:18:40,420 --> 00:18:42,980
There's no one girl that's buying lottery tickets,

462
00:18:42,980 --> 00:18:43,980
and there's probably not too many people

463
00:18:43,980 --> 00:18:46,420
consider a lottery ticket to be a good gift for,

464
00:18:46,420 --> 00:18:48,780
it's a lottery ticket for your infant child,

465
00:18:48,780 --> 00:18:51,780
with the dates being the kid's birthday.

466
00:18:51,780 --> 00:18:54,680
I think that's pretty odd, so that's a good one.

467
00:18:54,680 --> 00:18:57,300
All right, now, Thither goes on to say

468
00:18:57,300 --> 00:18:59,940
that to actually have a good chance of making money,

469
00:18:59,940 --> 00:19:01,220
you need to live 1,000 years,

470
00:19:01,220 --> 00:19:02,260
so we're playing the strategy,

471
00:19:02,260 --> 00:19:04,940
so don't get your hopes up too much,

472
00:19:04,940 --> 00:19:07,580
but still, interesting that a lot of people,

473
00:19:07,580 --> 00:19:10,940
these strategies do, can triple or quadruple,

474
00:19:10,940 --> 00:19:13,500
or even, you expect your returns,

475
00:19:13,500 --> 00:19:15,740
so out of all the people who do buy lottery tickets,

476
00:19:15,740 --> 00:19:17,580
why don't they do this?

477
00:19:17,580 --> 00:19:18,780
So you have a good idea.

478
00:19:18,780 --> 00:19:23,780
All right, so questions about this?

479
00:19:24,220 --> 00:19:25,900
Questions, yes?

480
00:19:25,900 --> 00:19:27,780
Is there any evidence in betting markets

481
00:19:27,780 --> 00:19:30,540
of people voting against upsetting outcomes

482
00:19:30,540 --> 00:19:32,440
in order to smooth their consumption?

483
00:19:33,540 --> 00:19:35,900
Let's say I would be really distressed

484
00:19:35,900 --> 00:19:38,540
if Trump got reelected, so I bet on Trump,

485
00:19:38,540 --> 00:19:40,460
so that either way, I'll get the money.

486
00:19:40,460 --> 00:19:41,300
So if anything, I'd say the evidence

487
00:19:41,300 --> 00:19:42,820
is the people bet on the outcomes they hope to happen

488
00:19:42,820 --> 00:19:45,540
because they feel guilty if they bet on other stuff.

489
00:19:45,540 --> 00:19:48,700
There's certainly a norm in sports

490
00:19:48,700 --> 00:19:51,460
that if you're gonna bet, don't bet against your own team.

491
00:19:54,020 --> 00:19:55,780
But Pete Rose got in trouble for this

492
00:19:55,780 --> 00:19:57,540
because he bet against his own team.

493
00:19:57,540 --> 00:19:59,140
Anyway, defenders will say there's no evidence

494
00:19:59,140 --> 00:20:26,680
that he actually pulled his money.


